.TH TONE 2
.SH NAME
tone: init, snd, beep, stop, play, voicenames, setvoice, playparts, settempo, sfx, sfxtick \- a small synthesiser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "tone.m";
tone := load Tone Tone->PATH;

Rate: con 22050;

init: fn(): string;
snd:  fn(freq: int);
beep: fn(freq, duration_ms: int);
stop: fn();
play: fn(score: string): string;

voicenames: fn(): list of string;
setvoice:   fn(name: string): string;
playparts:  fn(parts: array of string): string;
settempo:   fn(quarter_ms: int);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tone
.RB ( /dis/lib/tone.dis )
writes 16-bit stereo PCM to the device described in
.IR audio (3)
at
.B Tone->Rate
samples per second (22050 Hz).
.PP
The TempleOS programs this library was first written for drove a one-bit
square wave through a timer chip, so a note there is a frequency and
nothing else: full amplitude at the instant it starts, silence at the
instant it ends, one note at a time.  Inferno is under no such
obligation, and
.I tone
is not one now.  A voice is a harmonic series with an attack, a decay to
a sustain level, and a release; several voices play at once.
.PP
.B Init
does
.I not
open the audio device \(em opening it costs about 150ms on a hosted
emulator, and forty programs in this tree called
.B init
before putting their window up.  It loads what the library needs and
returns
.B nil
if the library is usable.  The device is opened on the first note, and a
failure to open it is reported once.
.PP
.B Snd
starts a continuous tone at
.I freq
Hertz.
A frequency of
.B 0
silences output (equivalent to turning the PC speaker off).
.B Beep
plays
.I freq
for
.I duration_ms
milliseconds and then stops.
If
.I duration_ms
is less than or equal to zero, the tone continues until
.B stop
is called.
.B Stop
silences immediately.
.PP
.B Play
interprets a compact score string.
Digits set the current octave (programs typically use 4 through 6).
The letters
.BR w ,
.BR h ,
.BR q ,
.BR e ,
and
.B s
select whole, half, quarter, eighth, and sixteenth durations.
Notes are
.B A
through
.BR G ,
with
.B #
and
.B b
accidentals;
.B R
is a rest.
An example used by several demos is
.BR 6hEqDC5B6CDhE .
.B Play
blocks while notes are emitted and returns
.B nil
or an error string.
.PP
.IR Asciiorgan (1)
maps typed keys to
.BR beep .
.IR Ohgreat (1)
and
.IR psalmody (1)
drive
.BR play .
Many games spawn a small loop that repeatedly
.B play s
background phrases while a pause flag is clear.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	if(tone->init() == nil){
		tone->beep(880, 100);
		tone->play("5qCEqG");
		tone->stop();
	}
.EE
.SH VOICES
.B Voicenames
returns the built-in voices, in order:
.BR sine ,
.BR organ ,
.BR string ,
.BR reed ,
.BR bell ,
.B pluck
and
.BR flute .
.B Setvoice
selects one and returns
.B nil
or a diagnostic naming what is available; it rebuilds the wavetable, so
call it between notes rather than during one.  The default is
.BR organ .
.B Sine
is the old behaviour, kept so that a caller can ask for it back.
.PP
Each voice is a list of harmonic amplitudes summed once into a
single-cycle wavetable when the voice is chosen, so a note costs a table
lookup and a linear interpolation per sample rather than one
.B sin
per harmonic per sample.  At 22050Hz with six harmonics and four parts
that is the difference between about half a million
.B sin
calls a second and none.
.SH POLYPHONY
.B Playparts
plays several scores at once, one per part, all starting together; each
part uses the same syntax as
.BR play ,
which is
.B playparts
with a single part.  The parts are mixed and the sum scaled by the square
root of their number.
.PP
.B Settempo
sets the quarter-note length in milliseconds for both, 500 (120bpm) by
default; the other durations scale with it.
.SH BUGS
.B Stop
does not interrupt a
.B play
or
.B playparts
already under way \(em it takes effect at the next note started by
.B snd
or
.BR beep .
.PP
A voice's envelope applies to every note of a score equally; there is no
per-note dynamic, and no way to slur two notes into one.
.PP
The harmonic amplitudes are fixed per voice rather than varying with
pitch, so the top of a voice's range is brighter than an instrument's
would be.
.SS One voice, several things wanting it
.I Beep
returns at once and the next one replaces whatever is sounding.
That is right for a program making one noise at a time and wrong for
anything with events in it: a footstep issued a frame after an explosion
cuts the explosion off.
.PP
.I Sfx
is an arbiter rather than a mixer, because there is nothing here to mix
with.
An effect states a priority and how long it lasts; one of lower priority
asked for while a louder one is still sounding is dropped, and one of
equal or higher priority takes over.
.I Sfxtick
must be called regularly \- once a frame, for a program that has frames
\- or a finished effect goes on holding the voice.
.PP
Both are silent until
.I init
has succeeded, so a program with no audio, or one running a self test,
makes no sound and spawns no process by simply not asking for any.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/tone.b
.br
.B /module/tone.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR audio (3),
.IR asciiorgan (1),
.IR ohgreat (1),
.IR psalmody (1),
.IR temple (1)
.SH BUGS
Only one voice is available.
